# Test: testing criteria (`y-tst-testing-criteria`)

Use this rule when a work item needs a **Testing Criteria** document that Tech and QA can use to judge pass/fail **without** prescribing specific test cases.

Product owners define **what must be true**; QA defines **how to test it** (see `.cursor/y-generative-search/y-generative-search-rules/test/y-tst-test-cases.mdc`).

## When to use

- Behavior changes in prompts, knowledge base, routing, or triage flows
- Domain-expert or PO review follow-ups that need explicit acceptance boundaries
- Handoff from implementation to QA before release sign-off

Do **not** use this rule for E2E run reports (use `.cursor/y-generative-search/y-generative-search-rules/insight/y-ins-e2e-report.mdc`) or conversation formatting (use `.cursor/y-generative-search/y-generative-search-rules/insight/y-ins-conversation.mdc`).

## Artifact location

- Save under **`docs/<JIRA-KEY>/testing-criteria.md`** (for example `docs/VAP-2291/testing-criteria.md`).
- One file per work item unless the user specifies a different path.
- Track the file in git when created for implementation work.

## Document title

```markdown
# <JIRA-KEY> — Testing Criteria
```

## Header block (required, immediately after H1)

Use this metadata block before the first section:

```markdown
**Work item:** [<JIRA-KEY>](https://vendoroo-dev.atlassian.net/browse/<JIRA-KEY>)
**Audience:** Tech and QA
**Purpose:** <One sentence: what “done and correct” means for this change.>
```

## Required structure (in this order)

Use **Title Case** for every section and subsection heading (`##` and `###`).

Do **not** include **Product Goal** or **Reference Implementation** sections.

---

### 1. Acceptance Criteria

Main section: `## Acceptance Criteria`

Organize criteria into numbered subsections:

```markdown
### 1. <Criterion Group Name>
```

Guidelines for each criterion group:

- Name the group by **observable behavior** (for example “Plumbing Routing Order”, “Overflow Photo Collection”).
- State the rule in plain language first when a short intro helps.
- Prefer a **Pass / Fail** table when multiple rows make scanning easier.
- When a single rule fits one row, you may use bold **Pass:** / **Fail:** lines instead of a table.
- Include brief **product or operational context** only when it clarifies *why* the criterion exists (one line under a table is enough).
- Do **not** list suggested test cases, scripts, or step-by-step test procedures in this document.

**Pass / Fail table shape (preferred):**

| Requirement | Pass | Fail |
|-------------|------|------|
| **<Short label>** | <Observable pass condition> | <Observable fail condition> |

**Routing or priority rules** may use a three-column table when order matters:

| Order | When This Applies | Required Behavior |
|-------|-------------------|-------------------|
| **First** | … | … |

Add as many numbered subsections (`### 1.` … `### N.`) as the work item needs.

Separate major criterion groups with `---` when it improves readability.

---

### 2. Definition of Done for Testing

Section: `## Definition of Done for Testing`

- Numbered checklist summarizing **acceptance criterion groups** (mirror `### 1.` … `### N.` titles or themes).
- State that **\<JIRA-KEY\> is accepted** when Tech and QA confirm the listed behaviors on representative conversations or flows.
- End with the **semantic equivalence** rule when wording flexibility matters:

  > No single exact phrase is required for pass—**semantic equivalence** is acceptable when the criterion is about natural language in assistant responses.

Adjust or omit the semantic-equivalence line when the work item is not conversation-based.

---

## Style

- **B2 English:** clear, professional, short sentences.
- **Audience:** Tech and QA; avoid implementation file paths and code-level detail in the criteria doc.
- **No test cases:** criteria only; derive scenarios in `.cursor/y-generative-search/y-generative-search-rules/test/y-tst-test-cases.mdc`.
- **Title Case:** all `##` and `###` headings (for example `## Acceptance Criteria`, `### 2. Overflow Photo Collection`).
- Use horizontal rules (`---`) between major blocks sparingly.

